.TH TKSPINBOX 2
.SH NAME
tkspinbox \- a number with an up and a down, out of the widgets there are
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "tk.m";
include "tkspinbox.m";
tkspinbox := load Tkspinbox Tkspinbox->PATH;

Spinbox: adt {
    top:   ref Tk->Toplevel;
    path:  string;
    lo:    int;
    hi:    int;
    step:  int;

    value: fn(sb: self ref Spinbox): int;
    set:   fn(sb: self ref Spinbox, v: int);
    event: fn(sb: self ref Spinbox, s: string): int;
};

init: fn();
new:  fn(top: ref Tk->Toplevel, path: string,
         lo, hi, step, start: int): (ref Spinbox, chan of string);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B New
makes a spinbox at
.I path
inside an existing toplevel: an entry holding a number, with a
.B +
and a
.B \-
beside it.
The caller packs
.I path
itself.
The value starts at
.I start
and is kept within
.I lo
and
.IR hi ,
inclusive; one press moves it by
.IR step .
If
.I hi
is below
.I lo
the two are exchanged, and a
.I step
of zero or less is taken as one.
.PP
.B Value
returns the number showing, and
.B set
puts one in, clamped.
Both read and write the entry itself rather than a copy, so a number the
person typed is the number
.B value
reports \(em and anything they typed that is not a number reads as
.IR lo .
.PP
.B New
also returns a channel.
The two buttons report on it, and an application must select on it and
give what arrives to
.BR event ,
which does the arithmetic and returns the value afterwards.
.B Event
ignores a string it does not recognise and returns the value unchanged,
so an application can hand it everything it receives without sorting
first.
.SS Why an application has to pump it
A Tk command is a string, and this Tk cannot add one to a number, so the
arithmetic has to happen in Limbo.
That leaves two arrangements: a process inside the module reading its own
channel, or the application's existing event loop.
The first would put a second writer on the same toplevel as the
application, and this is not the place to find out what that does.
.SS Why the value lives in an entry
Not a detail of appearance.
An entry is a control with a state, so
.B libtk
already announces it to
.IR voice (3)
when the keyboard arrives at it, and a person who tabs onto a spinbox
hears the number without this module knowing that speech exists.
A number drawn on a canvas would have been silent.
.PP
The up and down arrow keys are bound on the entry, so the widget can be
spun by someone who is not holding a mouse \(em the same person the
announcement below is for.
They report on the same channel as the buttons.
.PP
What an entry does not do is say the value changed when a
.I button
or a key
moved it, because nothing was typed into it.
.B Event
writes the new number to
.B /dev/voice/screen
itself \(em the only place in the module that mentions speech \(em and
only when the value actually moved, since at either end of the range a
press does nothing and repeating the same number at someone leaning on a
button is worse than silence.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/tkspinbox.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR tk (2),
.IR tknotebook (2),
.IR voice (3),
.IR entry (9),
.IR style (9)
.SH BUGS
Integers only.
.PP
It does not repeat while a button is held.
.PP
Nothing stops the person typing a number outside the range, or letters.
.B Value
clamps and falls back to
.I lo
so a caller is never handed nonsense, but the entry goes on showing what
they typed until something calls
.BR set .
